TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan / Preliminary Progress — launch, orbit and ground segment stages tied to course astrodynamics material and orbit calculations"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan — 'The design will be completed in STK (Systems Tool Kit), accessed remotely through the MECH lab 204 licence.'"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan — Gantt chart (Figure 1) with task descriptions and deadlines"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan — Table 2: Planned information sources"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress — orbit hand calculations, STK tutorials, and swath/revisit interpretation"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"10 m GSD weekly imaging of a 2300 km reef is a demanding requirement; data volume and downlink rate needed to meet the 24-hour delivery objective are not yet quantified (acknowledged as future work via contingencies)."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"Sun-synchronous orbit choice is not yet justified against local time of node, which matters for consistent illumination of colour-change detection."}],"overall":"Excellent preliminary report: well-justified Australian reef-monitoring mission with quantified objectives and users, a robust contingency-aware Gantt plan, correct verified orbit calculations with interpretation, and polished presentation. Minor gaps in trade justification and downlink sizing.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"Clear mission goal, four quantified objectives, explicit users and their needs, and objectives traced back to user requirements. Well referenced to literature ([1]-[6]). Only minor gap: no discussion of why a new satellite beats existing missions (e.g. Sentinel-2).","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef"},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"The satellite would be operated by the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS). Its customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"Gantt chart with task descriptions, explicit rationale linking plan order to mission logic, float, and a risk/contingency table with triggers and responses. Information sources table included. Robust and mission-integrated; only slight weakness is no stated weekly hour budget.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Project work is divided into weeks in the Gantt chart in Figure 1, and each task is described below it."},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"The order follows the mission: the imaging requirements (objectives 1 and 2) fix the orbit altitude and local time, so orbit work comes before ground control"},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Table 1: Project risks and contingency plans."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Verified hand calculations: a=6978 km gives T=5801 s (correct), 14.9 orbits/day, sun-sync inclination 97.8° at 600 km (correct), 24.2° ground-track shift using the sidereal day (correct). Results are interpreted (swath vs revisit trade) and tied to contingencies; STK tutorials and a scenario started. Clearly on track.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"Table 3: Preliminary hand calculations for the candidate orbit."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"Weekly coverage (objective 1) is therefore not guaranteed by one satellite at this altitude and resolution."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":18.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Logical structure with clear headings, labelled figures and tables with captions, consistent formatting, professional tone, and in-text citations ([1]-[11]) matching the reference list. Writing is clear and concise throughout. Very minor: some references lack full titles/author detail.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Figure 1: Project Gantt chart, Weeks 2 to 10 (gold = completed, blue = planned, red lines = report deadlines)."},{"where":"References","quote":"[7] J. R. Wertz, D. F. Everett, and J. J. Puschell, Eds., Space Mission Engineering: The New SMAD."}]}]}
